   $q$Which detail from Kobayashi's report, if added, would most strengthen the conclusion that the phones caused the difference in scores?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The science article that every student read was about ocean currents and was 900 words long.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"irrelevant-detail"},{"text":"Kobayashi has studied attention and memory in teenagers for more than ten years.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"appeal-to-authority"},{"text":"The two groups had earned nearly the same scores on a reading test given one week before the experiment.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Most of the students in the experiment had owned their own phones for at least two years.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"irrelevant-detail"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The two groups had earned nearly the same scores on a reading test given one week before the experiment.$q$, $q$If both groups started as equally strong readers, the score gap is more likely due to the phones than to differences between the students. Kobayashi's experience makes her credible but does not rule out other explanations for this particular result.$q$, 'draft', '42e9e6fb-6d67-5df5-9790-4d353b6e41c8')
