   $q$A reviewer warns that sentence (2) could be mistaken for a call to eliminate printed books entirely. Which revision best distinguishes the writer's claim from that more extreme position?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The district should make openly licensed digital textbooks the standard text for core science and history courses, while keeping low-cost printed copies available on request.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The district should think about whether digital textbooks might possibly work.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"hedged-no-position"},{"text":"Digital textbooks are the future, and printed textbooks belong to the past.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"vague-slogan"},{"text":"The district should stop using printed books in every course, starting immediately.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overstated-claim"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The district should make openly licensed digital textbooks the standard text for core science and history courses, while keeping low-cost printed copies available on request.$q$, $q$The correct revision names the courses and keeps printed copies available, which matches the supports described in sentence (9). The slogan about the future is memorable but does not state a precise, arguable claim.$q$, 'draft', '22a42c20-37e4-50da-bf6b-fa65ac05b899')
