insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('78c7f5e9-36b1-5db0-a259-60496079ef53', 'wg11-argument-writing-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-argument-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Darker Skies for Marrow Bay$q$, $q$(1) On a clear night, residents of Marrow Bay can see only a few dozen stars, while visitors who drive twenty miles inland can see thousands. (2) Marrow Bay should require that new and replaced outdoor lights be shielded to point downward and use warmer, lower-intensity bulbs. (3) Unshielded fixtures send much of their light sideways and upward, where it brightens the sky without making streets any safer. (4) Bright night skies also disrupt wildlife, and the town's beaches are a nesting site for sea turtles whose hatchlings crawl toward artificial light instead of the ocean. (5) Shielded fixtures direct light where people need it, so the town could use fewer watts and lower its electricity bills.

(6) Some residents fear that dimmer lighting will make streets less safe at night. (7) This fear is understandable. (8) Shielded lights reduce glare, which often makes it harder for drivers and walkers to see. (9) When the inland town of Corrigan switched to shielded fixtures, its police department reported no increase in nighttime incidents over the following three years. (10) Other residents say replacing lights would be expensive. (11) The rule would apply only when lights are installed or replaced, so no one would have to remove working fixtures.

(12) Stars are super pretty and everyone should get to see them. (13) Shielded lighting would protect the town's turtles and its view of the sky without making streets less safe or forcing costly replacements.$q$, 'draft')
