   $q$A student writes: "Each applicant should bring their portfolio to the interview." A teacher using a style guide from decades ago marks "their" as an error, while a current style guide accepts it. Which statement best explains the disagreement?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The sentence is wrong because \"applicant\" is a plural noun that needs \"they.\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misidentifies-number"},{"text":"Usage conventions shift over time, and many current guides accept \"their\" for one unnamed person.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The word \"their\" can never refer to a singular noun in any style guide.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralizes-old-rule"},{"text":"Pronoun rules never change, so one of the two guides must contain a printing mistake.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"usage-is-fixed"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Usage conventions shift over time, and many current guides accept "their" for one unnamed person.$q$, $q$Style guides record conventions, and those conventions change; many recent guides accept singular "their" when a person is unspecified, though older ones did not. The claim that rules never change ignores that guides are revised as usage develops.$q$, 'draft', null)
