   $q$A writer revises "Which club did you finally decide on?" to "On which club did you finally decide?" because an old handbook says never to end a sentence with a preposition. Which statement best reflects current usage guides?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Prepositions may end questions but never statements, so the revision was required.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"invented-rule"},{"text":"Most current guides accept a final preposition when rewording would sound stiff or unnatural.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Current guides require every sentence to end with a preposition in casual writing.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overcorrects-rule"},{"text":"Ending with a preposition is always a serious error, so the revision was required.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"treats-old-rule-as-fixed"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Most current guides accept a final preposition when rewording would sound stiff or unnatural.$q$, $q$Many modern usage guides treat the old ban on final prepositions as a preference rather than a rule and accept the original question as natural standard English. The claim that it is always a serious error reflects an older convention that has since relaxed.$q$, 'draft', null)
