insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ac862c71-796b-5d6a-ae3d-73f8ecea601d', 'wg11-paragraph-transitions-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-paragraph-transitions'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Voices of the Neighborhood$q$, $q$(1) Last spring, the public library in our town launched an oral history project to record the memories of longtime residents before those stories were lost. (2) Volunteers, most of them high school students, received training in interviewing, recording equipment, and the ethics of handling personal stories. (3) ____, each volunteer was paired with a resident who had agreed to share recollections of the neighborhood as it had been decades earlier.

(4) The interviews revealed details that no official record had captured. (5) One retired bus driver, Mr. Okonkwo, described the routes that once connected the riverside mills to the hillside neighborhoods, and he explained how the closing of the mills had reshaped those routes. (6) Another resident, Mrs. Lindqvist, recalled the weekly market that once filled the town square, where farmers, bakers, and knife sharpeners competed for customers' attention. (7) The library also offers free help with tax forms every winter. (8) Taken together, these accounts offered a picture of daily life far richer than the dates and statistics stored in the town archive.

(9) The project faced challenges as well. (10) Some residents hesitated to speak on record, worried that their memories were not important enough to preserve. (11) ____, transcribing hours of conversation proved far more time-consuming than the organizers had predicted. (12) Despite these obstacles, the volunteers completed more than forty interviews, which are now available to anyone who visits the library's local history room.$q$, 'draft')
