  ('e65e28e0-9af9-5b93-8f1a-807d12e7c179', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-precise-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Read the sentence: "The bridge finally opened after six years of planning, two funding shortfalls, and a complete redesign." Which revision builds suspense by holding the main clause until the end?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Six years of planning, two funding shortfalls, and a complete redesign, the bridge, it finally opened.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"redundant-subject"},{"text":"The bridge finally opened; it took six years of planning, two funding shortfalls, and a complete redesign.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"main-clause-early"},{"text":"After six years of planning, two funding shortfalls, and a complete redesign, the bridge finally opened.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The bridge, after six years of planning, finally opened, despite two funding shortfalls and a complete redesign.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"main-clause-early"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$After six years of planning, two funding shortfalls, and a complete redesign, the bridge finally opened.$q$, $q$Placing the list of obstacles first makes the reader wait for "the bridge finally opened," which gives the ending its weight. The semicolon version is correct but announces the opening right away.$q$, 'draft', null)
