   $q$Which of the following is a sentence fragment rather than a complete sentence?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Listen closely to the second movement of the symphony.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"imperative-misread-as-fragment"},{"text":"Beneath the stage sat a crate of old sheet music.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"inverted-sentence-misread"},{"text":"The violinist who had practiced the solo for months and still felt nervous.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The violinist practiced; the cellist rested.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"short-clauses-misread"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The violinist who had practiced the solo for months and still felt nervous.$q$, $q$In "The violinist who had practiced... and still felt nervous," both verbs belong to the relative clause, so "violinist" has no main verb. "Listen closely..." is complete because a command has an understood subject, "you."$q$, 'draft', null)
