   $q$Read the sentence: "The town's first railroad station opened in 1871 it served farmers shipping grain to the city, by 1900 it had become the busiest stop on the line." Which revision fixes every sentence boundary error?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The town's first railroad station opened in 1871; it served farmers shipping grain to the city, by 1900; it had become the busiest stop on the line.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misplaced-semicolon"},{"text":"The town's first railroad station opened in 1871 and served farmers shipping grain to the city, by 1900 it had become the busiest stop on the line.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"partial-fix-comma-splice-remains"},{"text":"The town's first railroad station opened in 1871 to serve farmers shipping grain to the city; by 1900, it had become the busiest stop on the line.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The town's first railroad station opened in 1871, it served farmers shipping grain to the city, by 1900 it had become the busiest stop on the line.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice-unfixed"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The town's first railroad station opened in 1871 to serve farmers shipping grain to the city; by 1900, it had become the busiest stop on the line.$q$, $q$Turning the second clause into the phrase "to serve farmers..." and placing a semicolon before "by 1900" leaves two properly joined independent clauses. The "and served" version fixes the first run-on but leaves a comma splice before "by 1900."$q$, 'draft', null)
